course: dictionary
lesson: 9784
title: Surround: all around
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To surround something is to be all around it, on every side. «The Arctic is a frozen ocean surrounded by land.» Land is on every side of that ocean, so surrounded means it has land all around it.
explain: Check the meaning by swapping in your idea. «A sea is a part of the ocean that is partly surrounded by land.» Partly means not all the way round, so a sea has land on some sides but not all. Your meaning still fits the sentence, so it is right.
explain: Surround can also mean moving so you end up all around someone or something. «In a lion pride the lionesses do most of the hunting, often working together to surround zebras.» The lionesses are not sitting still. They move until they are all around the zebras.
explain: Surround can be a noun too: a border or frame around something. A fireplace surround is the frame around the fire. Look at the words near it to decide whether surround is a verb (an action) or a noun (a thing).
fact: Surround means to be all around something on every side.
fact: «Surrounded by» is a useful phrase: an island is land surrounded by water.
fact: «The eye» of a tropical cyclone is a calm centre surrounded by a wall of the strongest winds.
fact: «Surround sound» is sound that seems to come at you from all around.
fact: A surround is a border or frame, like a fireplace surround.
exercise: «The Arctic is a frozen ocean surrounded by land.» What does surrounded mean here? | *Having land all around it on every side | Very deep under the water | Moving far away from the land | Covered with ice only
exercise: «In a lion pride the lionesses do most of the hunting, often working together to surround zebras.» What are the lionesses doing? | Hiding in the long grass away from the zebras | Chasing the lion cubs away | Sleeping in the shade | *Moving until they are all around the zebras
exercise: A fireplace surround is a frame around the fire. Is surround a verb or a noun in this phrase? | Adjective, because it describes the fire | *Noun, because it is a thing: a frame or border | Verb, because it is an action | Adverb, because it tells how the fire burns
test: «The eye of a tropical cyclone is a calm centre surrounded by a wall of the strongest winds.» What is the calm centre surrounded by? | *A wall of the strongest winds | Calm, still air | Ice from the Arctic | The lionesses' hunting ground
test: «The Oort cloud is a vast shell of icy bodies thought to surround the Solar System.» What does surround mean here? | *Lie all around, like a shell | Fly away from | Be the same size as | Hide from the light
exam: What is a surround? | *A border or frame around something, like a fireplace | A kind of ocean that is always frozen | A sound that comes from one speaker only | A group of wolves moving in a pack
